Part 2 -- ADT Questions
Q1. What does ADT stands for?
Abstract Data Type.

Q2. In your own words, what is an Abstract Data Type?
It basically describes what data structure should do and what operations it should have.

Q3. What is the difference between an ADT and its Implementation?
ADT describes WHAT data structure should do and Implementation is HOW data structure is actually built.

Q4. Can two programmers create different implementations of the same ADT?
Yes, as long as it follows same rules and operations.

Q5. If one Programmer creates a stack using an array and another creates a Stack using a linked list,
are both still Stacks? Explain Why.
Yes, because they both follow Stack rules. Implementation is different but ADT is still same.


Part 8 -- Stack Questions
Q6. What does LIFO means?
Last In First Out.

Q7. Why did 55 get removed before 15?
Because 55 was added last and in stack the last added element get removed first.

Q8. If stack contains A B C D and D was added last, which item should pop() remove first?
D

Q9. Give one real-world or software example where Stack could be useful?
A Stack can be used for a browser's Back button. The most recently visited page is the first page you
return to when you press Back.


Part 14 -- Queue Questions
Q10. What does FIFO mean?
First In First Out.

Q11. Why was 15 removed before 55?
Because 15 was added first and in queue the first element added gets removed first.

Q12. If customers enter a line in this order: Alex Maria John Sarah, who would leave Queue first?
Alex.

Q13. Give one real-world or software example where Queue could be useful?
A queue could be used at grocery stores where the first comes first served at tyhe checkout.


Part 15 -- Stack vs Queue
